# The closed-loop calibration run, start to finish, on the server.
#
# In v2.0 this was a chain of timers in the client's main window: shoot positive
# five times, shoot negative five times, clean, update the table, save, move Z
# back 20 mm, repeat for five sub-folders -- with a chessboard step first that
# pushes X out 20 mm and back. Every link of that chain is a rig command or a
# calculation, and both now live here.
#
# It also stops being fragile in a way that mattered: with the loop in the client,
# closing the window mid-run left the stage wherever the last move put it. The
# loop is now on the same machine as the stage, and a client that disappears does
# not abandon a moving axis -- the run continues, and reconnecting picks the
# feedback back up.

# Where the results go, RELATIVE TO THE SERVER's sessions root. Not a client
# path: the client's C: drive is not this machine's.
string run_name

# Sub-folder names, one per round. The historical set is 1, 3, 5, 7, 9.
string[] rounds

# Shots per pattern polarity, per round. Historically 5.
int32 shots_per_polarity

# Seconds between shots. Historically 10 -- long enough for the panel to settle.
float64 shot_interval

# Millimetres to move Z back between rounds. Historically 20.
float64 z_step_mm

# Run the chessboard step first: capture at the origin, then 20 mm right, then
# 20 mm left. It has to happen before the pattern rounds, because every pattern
# shot covers all five screens.
bool chessboard_step
float64 chessboard_offset_mm

# Camera name, used for the chessboard sub-folder.
string camera_name

# The patterns to put on the glass, as ShowPatternSpec JSON. The server renders
# them at each panel's own resolution; it does not invent them, because what is
# on the glass is the operator's calibration design and it is authored in the
# Pattern Generator.
#
# Four specs, because a round is four different pictures:
string spec_top_positive     # concentric, positive colours  -> TOP
string spec_side_positive    # stripeline, positive colours  -> N/W/S/E
string spec_top_negative
string spec_side_negative

# Chessboard, for the step that runs before the rounds. Empty disables that step
# regardless of `chessboard_step`.
string spec_chessboard_top
string spec_chessboard_side

# The 75 pattern values (25 concentric radii + 50 stripeline intervals) that fill
# the round table's PCT column. They describe the pattern above, so they travel
# with it -- a table computed against different radii than the glass was showing
# is wrong in a way no later step can detect.
string[] pct_values

# The scalar form inputs the pipeline reads (pixel sizes, gaps, distances), as
# the same JSON object CaliTableData carries under "fields".
string fields_json
---
bool success

# Where it wrote, on the server.
string run_path

# The completed table -- all rounds, as CaliTableData JSON, exactly what
# /compute/cali returns.
#
# Without this the run is invisible to the operator. The results are written on
# the rig, and XlsxIo deliberately moves BYTES rather than reading server paths,
# so a client that only got `run_path` back would have no way to show what the
# rig computed -- and the step that fills the camera Parameter panel
# (ControllerCaliResult::finishAutoCalibration) reads the client's round tables,
# which in the old client-driven run were populated as a side effect of Update
# Table. It costs nothing to send: the run already holds every round in one
# CaliTableData, because each round is added to the same object and computeAll
# runs over all of them.
string table_json

# Rounds actually completed. Fewer than requested after a cancel.
string[] completed

bool cancelled

# On abort mid-chessboard-step, the run undoes the X excursion -- that offset was
# temporary. The per-round Z moves are real progress and are left where they are.
# This says where the stage was left, because the next thing the operator does
# depends on it.
string final_pose

string message
---
# Which round, and where inside it. `stage` is the text the button shows:
# "[3] Pos 2/5", "[3] moving Z", "chessboard 1/3".
int32 round_index
int32 round_total
int32 done
int32 total
string stage

# The shot just taken, small, so the operator can watch the run rather than
# trust it. Empty on the non-capture steps.
sensor_msgs/CompressedImage preview
